\section{A Grammar for Machine Operands}

The tree examined by a selector describes a computation, not the syntax of the input language. Its terminals are IR operators, including typed constants and reads of existing temporaries. A \emph{tile} covers a connected pattern at a node; nonterminal leaves in the pattern describe what the selector must provide at its frontier. The same subtree can be covered as a register value, as an encodable immediate, or as an effective address. These are different promises to a parent instruction, even when all three denote the same integer. A rule that returns an address descriptor need not emit any code: the descriptor may become fields of the instruction that consumes it.

Aho and Johnson's \emph{Optimal Code Generation for Expression Trees} gives a dynamic-programming treatment of generation for trees without common subexpressions. Fraser, Henry, and Proebsting's \emph{BURG: Fast Optimal Instruction Selection and Tree Parsing} presents a generator that accepts a cost-augmented tree grammar. Here we isolate the covering problem from register allocation and take the costs to be additive. The rule cover determines which virtual operands and instructions are presented to later passes; it need not determine their final physical registers or order.

\section{Label Bottom Up, Recover from the Root}

For each tree node $v$ and goal nonterminal $g$, store a cost $C(v,g)$ and the rule that achieves it. A structural match of rule $r$ yielding $g$ gives a frontier $(v_i,g_i)$, the pairs of child subtrees and required operand forms. Then
\[
 C(v,g) = \min_{r\text{ matching }v\text{ for }g}
              \left(w(r)+\sum_i C(v_i,g_i)\right).
\]
The minimum over no viable alternatives is infinity. For rules whose frontier consists of proper descendants, the invariant is that a postorder traversal has already found the cheapest derivation of every frontier goal. The proof cuts any derivation at its root tile: disjoint frontier subtrees cannot change one another's local choices. Conversely, joining their cheapest derivations to a legal root tile constructs a derivation of exactly the computed cost. The objective and the independence assumption are both essential; picking the largest matching tile does not establish either.

A chain rule such as $A\leftarrow R$ changes the operand form at the same node. Close the node's table under such rules before visiting parents. With nonnegative costs one can treat the nonterminals as a small shortest-path graph, seeded with the structural costs. Use a well-founded tie break, such as fewer chain transitions, so that zero-cost cycles cannot appear in the recovered derivation. Starting with the goal at the root, descend through stored choices, emitting a rule's action when its inputs are ready. Don't run actions during labeling: a cheap-looking rule that loses a later comparison must leave no instructions behind.

\section{Worked Cover: Scaled Index Load}

Consider a toy target with goals $R$ for a register, $A$ for a memory address, $I$ for an immediate, and $S$ for the literal scale four. A temporary derives $R$ free and the constant four derives both $S$ and $I$ free. The operations below compute a wrapping address of the target width; assume any extra register restrictions have been checked.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{llr}
Goal & Pattern & Cost \\
$R$ & $\mathrm{mul}(R,I)$ & 3 \\
$R$ & $\mathrm{add}(R,R)$ & 2 \\
$A$ & $R$ & 0 \\
$A$ & $\mathrm{add}(R,\mathrm{mul}(R,S))$ & 0 \\
$R$ & $\mathrm{load}(A)$ & 2
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

For $\mathrm{load}(\mathrm{add}(b,\mathrm{mul}(i,4)))$, the address subtree costs five as $R$, and zero as $A$ via the scaled tile. The load costs two rather than seven. Changing the four to a three that still derives $I$ makes the scaled tile inapplicable; the chain route now costs five as $A$, and the full cover costs seven. Check not only the computed minimum but also the recovered tile sequence for both inputs. Adding a syntactically larger load tile with cost eight would make a largest-tile policy pick eight in the first case, where the dynamic program still picks two.

