\section{Close Chain Rules Without Recording a Cycle}

A cost table at one node can contain a legal structural match
only for a goal other than the one requested. Treat a chain
rule $A\leftarrow R$ as a directed edge from $R$ to $A$.
Seed the graph with all finite structural costs. With nonnegative
rule weights, use a shortest-path search ordered by the pair
of total cost and number of chain steps. The latter is not a
machine cost: it chooses a finite derivation consistently when
costs tie. Keep the winning predecessor alongside each pair.

\begin{sudocode}
\Require Structural table $S$, chain rules $E$
\Let{D}{\operatorname{seedPairs}(S,0)}
\Let{Q}{\operatorname{priorityQueueOfFiniteEntries}(D)}
\While{$Q$ is not empty}
  \Let{(g,k)}{\operatorname{popMinimum}(Q)}
  \If{$k=D[g]$}
    \To{Stale queue entries cannot replace the current record.}
    \ForAll{rules $g\to h$ of weight $w$ in $E$}
      \Let{z}{k+(w,1)}
      \If{$z<D[h]$ in lexicographic order}
        \To{Even a free edge increases the step count.}
        \State Record $z$ and predecessor $g$ for $h$
        \State Push $(h,z)$ into $Q$
      \EndIf
    \EndFor
  \EndIf
\EndWhile
\State \Return $D$ and the recorded choices
\end{sudocode}

At a toy node, seed $R$ with structural cost $5$ and $I$
with structural cost $7$. Let the edges $R\to A$ and
$A\to R$ both cost zero, and let $A\to I$ cost two.
The resulting entries for $R$, $A$, and $I$ are $(5,0)$,
$(5,1)$, and $(7,0)$. In particular, the free cycle offers
$(5,2)$ for $R$ and must not replace the structural match.
The path through $A$ offers $(7,2)$ for $I$, which ties
in cost but loses on steps. If the structural $I$ match
is absent, the path instead wins and recovers through $A$
and $R$.

Recovering each chain predecessor decreases its step count
by one, so recovery cannot loop. Unreachable goals retain
infinite cost and have no rule to emit. This closure handles
only transitions at one node; structural matches must
already incorporate their frontier costs. If the cost model
permits negative weights, this priority-queue algorithm no
longer gives the stated guarantee. A negative cycle could make
the minimum undefined rather than merely introduce a tie.

\section{Moving Work into the Generator}

Kang and Choe's study of bottom-up tree pattern matching describes states containing minimum-cost rules and costs normalized relative to the least-cost derivation. For suitable grammars, a table-driven matcher can precompute states and transitions indexed by opcode and descendant states, moving work to selector generation. Value-dependent tests such as displacement range still need a representation, for example by classifying constants before matching. Compare a generated matcher with the explicit dynamic program on small trees: reachability, minimum costs, and agreement of the emitted code with the IR are separate checks.

The cost proof assumes that every selected tile is itself correct. In the load example, that means not just computing the same address bits but retaining the access width and memory effects of the IR load. One can check each tile as a local contract parameterized by its frontier operands; the dossier's \href{https://github.com/Chubek/TheTomeOfLangDev/blob/HEAD/dossier-on-modern-cc-design/chapters/29-proof-carrying-ir-and-certified-rewrite-contracts.md}{Proof-Carrying IR and Certified Rewrite Contracts} develops this idea. Shared subexpressions break the disjoint-frontier argument for the cost proof; Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-222} takes up that case.
